 # Distributed Training and Sharding
 
-Also called: Distributed 3DGS, Multi-GPU Sharding
+Also called: Multi-GPU 3D Gaussian Splatting, Distributed 3DGS, Sharded Gaussian Training
 
 ## Overview
-Distributed training and sharding partition the global set of 3D Gaussians $\mathcal{G}$ and optimizer states across $M$ GPUs to enable city-scale optimization that exceeds single-GPU memory and compute limits [S2, unofficial]. Unlike spatial block partitioning, which suffers from cross-block visibility waste, imbalanced shards, and expensive merge steps, BlitzGS uses index-parity sharding combined with all-to-all communication to route Gaussians to responsible tiles, ensuring balanced loads without final merging [S2, unofficial].
+Distributed Training and Sharding enables the training and rendering of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) models that exceed the memory capacity of a single GPU by partitioning Gaussian primitives, camera views, or image tiles across multiple GPUs [S1][S2, unofficial][S3]. Unlike single-GPU rendering, which is limited by VRAM and compute throughput, this family of methods scales to city-scale or scientific datasets by distributing the workload [S1][S2, unofficial]. What sets this family apart from its siblings is the specific strategy for partitioning the global state: [BlitzGS](15088-blitzgs.md) uses index-parity sharding to avoid spatial boundary redundancy, [Distributed 3D Gaussian Splatting](16979-distributed-3d-gaussian-splatting.md) partitions primitives for scientific volume visualization, [Grendel](19770-grendel.md) employs mixed Gaussian-wise and pixel-wise parallelism with dynamic load balancing, and [Distributed Batched Rendering Loop](18495-distributed-batched-rendering-loop.md) focuses on batched view processing with all-to-all communication.
 
 ## Shared principles
-See [Hardware and Computational Acceleration](16214-hardware-and-computational-acceleration.md) for the shared rendering pipeline principles.
+The methods share a foundation in 3D Gaussian primitives defined by mean $\mu$, covariance $\Sigma$ (derived from scale $s$ and rotation $q$), opacity $\alpha$, and color/radiance parameters. Rendering involves projecting these 3D primitives to 2D screen space, typically via affine approximation of the projective transformation. The core mathematical operation is the evaluation of the Gaussian density function $P(x|\mu, \Sigma) = \exp\left(-\frac{1}{2}(x - \mu)^\top \Sigma^{-1} (x - \mu)\right)$ and alpha blending. Acceleration often relies on tiling the image plane and sorting primitives by depth.
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### BlitzGS
-BlitzGS is a distributed 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) framework designed to accelerate city-scale training by reducing the active Gaussian workload at three coupled levels: system-level sharding, model-level population control, and view-level filtering [S2, unofficial]. Unlike spatial block partitioning which suffers from cross-block visibility waste and imbalanced shards, BlitzGS shards Gaussians by index parity and uses a single all-to-all communication step to distribute rendering tiles, ensuring balanced loads without a final merge step [S2, unofficial]. It achieves nearly an order-of-magnitude speedup over an edge GPU baseline (5.41×) and up to 17.3× with a customized accelerator, while incurring minimal visual quality degradation [S1].
+### [BlitzGS](15088-blitzgs.md)
+BlitzGS is a distributed 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) framework designed to accelerate city-scale reconstruction by reducing the active Gaussian workload at system, model, and view levels. Unlike spatial block partitioning which suffers from cross-block redundancy and imbalance, BlitzGS shards Gaussians by index parity and couples this with importance-based pruning and distance-based LOD filtering to achieve near-order-of-magnitude training speedups.
 
 ### [GS-Cache Inference Framework](15588-gs-cache-inference-framework.md)
-GS-Cache is a holistic rendering framework designed to support real-time rendering of large-scale 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) models at the city level, achieving >72 FPS at binocular 2K resolution. It addresses the computational bottleneck of the "Gaussian Derivation Stage" in structured Gaussian methods (e.g., Scaffold-GS, Octree-GS) by employing a cache-centric de-redundancy pipeline. Unlike native 3DGS or unstructured methods, GS-Cache specifically targets structured representations where anchors derive neural Gaussians, optimizing inter-frame redundancy in parameter decoding while maintaining rasterization quality.
+GS-Cache is a holistic rendering framework designed to support real-time rendering of large-scale 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) models at the city level, achieving >72 FPS at binocular 2K resolution by using a cache-centric de-redundancy pipeline and elastic parallel scheduling. It specifically targets 'Structured Gaussian derivation methods' (like Scaffold-GS and Octree-GS) which use anchors to derive neural Gaussians, addressing the bottleneck of the 'Gaussian Derivation Stage' (decoding anchor features) which dominates computation time. Unlike native 3DGS or unstructured methods, GS-Cache optimizes the derivation stage by caching decoded parameters, while leaving the rasterization stage uncached to preserve visual quality against occlusion changes.
+
+### [Distributed 3D Gaussian Splatting](16979-distributed-3d-gaussian-splatting.md)
+Distributed 3D Gaussian Splatting is a multi-GPU extension of the 3D-GS pipeline designed for scientific visualization, enabling the scalable processing of large datasets that exceed the memory capacity of a single GPU. It differs from standard 3D-GS by partitioning the global set of Gaussian primitives and training data across multiple GPUs, utilizing a fused all-reduce scheme for gradient synchronization to maintain high-fidelity isosurface reconstruction at scales previously infeasible.
+
+### [Distributed Batched Rendering Loop](18495-distributed-batched-rendering-loop.md)
+A distributed rendering pipeline that processes camera views in batches, divides workload across multiple GPUs, preprocesses 3D Gaussian Splatting data, performs all-to-all communication, renders, and aggregates results across ranks. It sets itself apart from single-GPU rendering by enabling high-resolution and large-scale 3D reconstruction tasks that exceed single GPU memory capacity, and from non-batched distributed systems by supporting parallel processing of multiple camera views to improve GPU utilization.
+
+### [Online Image Rendering Placement](19195-online-image-rendering-placement.md)
+Online Image Rendering Placement is a per-iteration optimization step in distributed 3D Gaussian Splatting training that assigns image patches to GPUs to minimize inter-node communication and balance computational load. Unlike offline graph partitioning which clusters both points and images for the entire dataset, this method dynamically assigns rendering tasks for a small random batch of images at each training step, adapting to the changing frustum visibility of the scene. It distinguishes itself from static sharding by using a fast heuristic solver (Linear Sum Assignment followed by local search) to solve an Integer Linear Programming (ILP) problem within the latency constraints of online training.
+
+### [Grendel](19770-grendel.md)
+Grendel is a distributed training system for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that partitions parameters and parallelizes computation across multiple GPUs to overcome single-GPU memory constraints in high-resolution and large-scale reconstruction. It distinguishes itself by supporting batched training with multiple views and exploiting mixed parallelism (Gaussian-wise and pixel-wise) with dynamic load balancing, whereas conventional systems like CityGS use a divide-and-conquer approach with coarse training and cell merging.
 
 ### LS-Gaussian Accelerator Architecture
-**Definition:** A hardware architecture co-designed with the LS-Gaussian algorithm to support workload-aware mapping, featuring a streaming pipeline that allows continuous processing across hardware modules without requiring global synchronization [S1].
-**Step:** The accelerator predicts the workload for each tile based on viewpoint transformation to enable more balanced parallel computation [S1].
-**Comparison:** Unlike naive assignment of tiles to rendering blocks which fails to achieve balanced load distribution, this architecture uses workload prediction to mitigate inter-block idling and intra-block bubbles [S1].
